def rule_a_distilled_rule_must_be_routed_into_a_program_some_verdict_consults(sample_text, ports):
    """Routing a rule to the program that owns its subject is necessary and not sufficient: the program must also be one that something invokes. A rule in a program no verdict consults is indistinguishable, from every artefact the capture produces, from a rule that fires and finds nothing — and it is worse than an unwritten rule, because the record asserts the class is now covered. Join the routing table to the wiring census at emit time and refuse the pairing, rather than discovering it when a later run does not catch the thing the record promised."""
    # Expected signal: ERROR
    # Suggested fix action: At emit time, resolve each record's target program through the routing table and check it against the wiring census the tree already maintains — the one that lists gates no automatic verdict consults, together with its standing baseline. Refuse a record whose target is unwired, naming both the record and the program, and say which of the two must change: wire the program, or route the rule to one that runs. MEASURED on this batch, and RE-MEASURED after it grew: 26 Bucket-A records resolve to 16 distinct programs and NONE of them is unwired (it was 21 into 15 when the rule was written; re-running the check is the rule applying to itself), so the batch passes the check it is proposing — which is the result to want and not the one to assume, because I did not verify it until I wrote this rule. The census on this tree reports 619 gates with 61 unwired against a baseline of 59, and names three as newly unwired; one of those three is a program of the very layer this batch is about, so the pairing was reachable rather than hypothetical. TWO SELF-INSTANCES, both found in this batch's own verifier and both of the shape 'the artefact is present and the thing that should consume it consumes something else'. (1) A check appended at the end of the verifier runs AFTER the verdict has read its failure list and exited: it prints, it looks green, and it cannot fail the run. Two checks were written that way and were dead until a third refused them. (2) One helper name was defined twice; callers bind at call time, so every call after the second definition silently got the other body -- the intended normaliser scored 0.385 on the duplicate guard and the shadow 0.361, and the shadow is what any newly-appended check would have used. The rule generalises past routing tables: a guard must be shown to be REACHED by the verdict that reports it, and a name must resolve to one body, or the guard is decoration with a passing label. SWEPT over a population larger than this batch, and it survives. The earlier figure measured this lane's own records, which tests compliance rather than the rule's false positives. The rule's real population is every capture record in the repository: 53 Bucket-A records across three lanes, 23 of them written by other agents on other clusters. Result: 0 steps with no routing entry and 0 routed at a program no automatic verdict consults -- no false positives and, on this tree, no true ones either. Two things qualify that. The clean result is CONDITIONAL on the routing entries this branch adds: without them 9 of this lane's 30 records would have no entry at all, so the rule would fire nine times on a tree that had merely not been told where the work belongs. And a rule firing on nothing is still worth building, for the reason its clean sibling already gives -- the state it protects was reached by hand, and nothing keeps the next lane inside it. THE SCREEN: the figure is not counted by hand -- it is the number the wiring gate itself prints on its first line when run with no arguments, which is the same enumeration the rule is about. Re-run today it prints 624 against the 619 recorded, a growth of five. Taking the number from the gate rather than from an independent count is deliberate: an independent count would have to re-derive what counts as a gate, and a second definition of the population is exactly how two figures about one thing start disagreeing.
