# Auto-captured by benchmark-enhancement-capture at plugin v1.11.69
# Pattern: A rule is distilled from a defect, and to size it the author writes a screen over the tree and quotes what it returns. The screen is written from the same understanding that produced the rule, so it inherits the same blind spot: it matches something ADJACENT to the subject. Nothing catches this, because a population count has no expected value to compare against — the number is the answer, so any number looks like the answer. The failure is silent in both directions, and the direction that survives review is the LOW one: a screen returning a small tidy number reads as a well-scoped rule, while a screen returning an absurd number at least invites suspicion.
# CORPUS-SWEEP REQUIRED before merging: zero false-positives across
# the open-benchmark corpora used by `score_iverilog_tb.py`.

def rule_a_quoted_population_count_must_name_the_known_instance_its_screen_was_validated_(sample_text, ports):
    """A count over a population is a measurement, and a measurement whose apparatus was never checked against a known reading is not evidence. Before quoting what a screen returns, run it against an instance already known to be in the population — the defect the rule came from is always available and costs nothing — and state that it was found. A screen that cannot find the case it was written for is not narrow, it is wrong, and its number is unusable in either direction."""
    # Expected signal: ERROR
    # Suggested fix action: At emit time, require a record that quotes a population count to also name the known instance the screen located, and refuse the record when it does not. The control is free: the motivating defect is by construction a member. Prefer two runs in opposite directions — a broad form and a narrow one — because a single number is unfalsifiable while a pair that disagree by an order of magnitude localises the error immediately. MEASURED ON THIS BATCH, WHICH IS THE EVIDENCE: twelve screens I wrote returned a number I could not use. A prefix match pulled in every permission flag, 34 for 9. A verb matched a path three paragraphs away, 560 for 0. A no-argument probe measured the argument parser, 18 of 19 and worthless. Module-stem matching counted in-repo packages as third-party, 216 for 51. An exit code read after a pipe reported the status of the last command in it. One matched a pattern literal inside a regex and reported a document that does not exist. AND THREE OF THE TWELVE FAILED TO FIND THE VERY CASE THEY WERE WRITTEN FOR — a pattern held in a constant, a command spanning continuation lines, and a name composed by a format string — which a positive control would have caught in one run each. The warnings are currently scattered across five records under five headings, so a reader of any one of them sees only their local instance; this record is the class. AND THE CONTROL MUST BE ABLE TO FAIL, which this rule did not say and which cost it a published number. A control that compares a thing to ITSELF passes on a broken instrument, because identity survives any defect; and a control run through the same call path as the measurement inherits the fault it is meant to detect. Require SEPARATION instead: the instrument must score a deliberately-similar case high AND an unrelated case low, with the gap stated. MEASURED — a similarity check in this batch reported a maximum of 0.07 and was published; the matcher had a heuristic enabled that discards frequent characters on long inputs, disabled only by a constructor argument that was set afterwards where it does nothing. With the instrument built correctly the same comparison reads 0.38 over a larger population, the near-duplicate control reads 0.966 against an unrelated baseline of 0.144, and the conclusion survives — but the first number was produced by an instrument that could not see the thing it measured. TWO SELF-INSTANCES, both found by reading this report rather than by running its verifier, and both in records this same batch wrote. The provenance-path record quoted a split and an argument built on it; re-measured independently, the totals did not reproduce and the ARGUMENT inverted -- the split is mixed within producers, not clean between them -- and the only reason the disagreement was resolvable at all is that the underlying trees were still present to re-count. The declared-output-path record quotes a four-level breakdown with no screen recorded at all, so it cannot be re-derived even in principle. Neither was caught by thirty-nine mechanical checks, because a number with no stated screen is not inconsistent with anything -- there is nothing for a check to compare it to. That is the strongest form of this rule's argument: the defect is invisible to verification BY CONSTRUCTION, so the screen has to be recorded at the moment of measurement or the figure is permanently unfalsifiable. A THIRD SELF-INSTANCE, and the one that shows the rule is not only about counts. Two guards were probed with synthetic inputs, both returned PASS, and both PASSes were written down as properties of the guards. Neither probe could see its subject: one guard chooses its population from a switch that was not passed, and the other's population is programs that make a particular statement, which the stub never made. Rebuilt with a two-arm control -- one input that commits the offence and one that does not -- both guards fire and both name the offending arm. The rule generalises from a quoted count to any measurement: validate the instrument against a KNOWN-POSITIVE instance before reporting what it did not find, because a probe that cannot see its subject and a subject that is clean produce the same output. Following it here would have prevented two wrong statements in two consecutive commits. A THIRD SELF-INSTANCE, found while trying to re-measure the batch's older sweeps against a tree that had moved thirty commits. Three of those rows re-measured cleanly because they stated how they were counted, and one did not: its two figures are bare labels, and a plausible reconstruction returned more than twice each of them. That difference is the whole argument. Had the tree genuinely moved that far the record would deserve a correction; because the screen is unknown, the only honest reading is that two different screens produced two different numbers and neither can be compared to the other. An unrecorded screen does not merely make a figure unverifiable -- it makes every later attempt to check it produce a CONFLICTING figure that looks like drift.
    return []  # list of findings — TODO implement

