# Pattern: An emitter that takes an output directory as a free string accepts any directory, so the convention that decides whether the bundle is found later lives only in prose. Detect by requiring the output directory to match the declared layout for that artefact class, and refuse a path that does not.
# CORPUS-SWEEP REQUIRED before merging: zero false-positives across
# the open-benchmark corpora used by `score_iverilog_tb.py`.

def rule_an_emitted_bundle_must_be_written_to_the_declared_location(sample_text, ports):
    """An emitted bundle is written to the declared location.

An output directory supplied as a free string is not a location, it is a suggestion. When the layout that consumers rely on is written only in prose, each producer picks its own directory and every one of them is self-consistent. Require the path to match the declared layout and refuse the rest, so the disagreement is caught at emit time rather than by a human moving directories afterwards."""
    # Expected signal: emit refuses an output directory outside the declared layout, naming the expected form and the path it was given
    # Suggested fix action: The emitter takes --out-dir as an arbitrary string and validates nothing about it. MEASURED, and not by me: the commit that introduced the canonical layout (506ff68c1) records that of FOUR lanes emitting the same artefact class on the same night, THREE chose a different home -- two at the repo root, one correct -- and a fifth wrote a bare RESULT.md at the root whose name collides by construction because three lanes wrote one. All four were repaired by hand with git mv. This lane was one of the three wrong ones and did not discover it from the tree: the branch was measured against a base that had since moved, and the relocation only surfaced because the merge reported a rename. Nothing in programs/ mentions the layout at all -- grep for it returns no checker, so the next lane will land in the same place. BUILD: give the emitter the declared layout for a capture bundle (docs/capture/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<agent>/candidates), compare the resolved --out-dir against it, and exit non-zero with both the expected form and the given path when they differ. The predicate is a path comparison, the population is every emit invocation, and the refusal is an exit code -- no judgment anywhere in it. Do NOT infer the layout from where other bundles happen to sit; that is the same prose-convention failure one level up. Declare it once, in a constant the checker and the emitter both read. A SECOND CLAUSE, stated here rather than left implicit, because a bundle has an identity as well as a location and nothing checks either. This bundle's directory declares one date and its own summary declares the next one, because the batch was re-emitted after midnight and the emitter derives its date from a single instant at run time -- correctly, since a record filed today should say today. The two sibling bundles landed the same night agree with themselves; this one is the only mismatch, and nothing anywhere would have reported it. It is NOT repaired by renaming the directory: that would abandon the path this bundle already occupies on the landing branch and create a second copy, which is the first clause's failure. So the rule has two halves: the bundle is written to the declared location, AND the identity its contents assert agrees with the identity that location declares, or the discrepancy is stated where a reader will meet it. The predicate compares the date in the directory name against the date in the emitted summary, the population is every bundle under the capture root, and the refusal names both dates.
    return []  # list of findings — TODO implement

